		Three dimensional spectrogram display program
		---------------------------------------------

Author: Alan W. Peevers, E-mu Systems, Scotts Valley, CA.

Spectrogram is a program which resulted from my MS project at UC
Berkeley during the winter of 1993-94. As such, it is being offered
to the world at large so that people can have fun with it. There is
absolutley NO support for this program, and no responsibility is
assumed by the author, SGI, or the University of California for any
losses incurred through its use. There are many bugs and inaccuracies, 
some of which I know about, and others I may not.  But the program is 
fun and useful as is, as an educational tool, as long as you agree to
these terms.

For the impatient:
------------------
1) It's nice to have a sounds directory, with some .aiff files in it,
   in your current directory.
2) Set the sample rate to something low (for speech, 8-11 kHz works
   well).
3) Say spectrogram -l (for 'live' input), or spectrogram with no args
   to bring up a list of your .aiff files. Don't load a Huge soundfile
   or it will gobble up ALL of your system's memory and page like mad!
4) If you want usage, say spectrogram -h (for help). 

This program displays spectrograms of audio data on the SGI screen in
3D. The dimensions of the spectrogram are x<-->frequency, y<-->amplitude,
and z<-->time. This type of image is very useful in analyzing and
understanding signals, for example in speech and acoustics research.
Conventional spectrograms just use greyscale to depict the amplitude
dimension. This program attempts to show that by using a true 3D
display, more information can be conveyed. 

It has a rich set of command line options (issue -h to see them), with
reasonable defaults for all of them. When invoked with no options, it
will bring up a menu of files in the current 'sounds' directory (if
you don't have a sounds directory, you can modify the directory field
[click on it with the mouse] to point to where your aiff files are).

Once you've opened a sound file,the display can be manipulated using
mouse movements to tilt the display to the desired orientation.  When
the middle mouse button is depressed, you will see a rectangular grid
displayed on the screen.  This represents the 'baseline' onto which
the 3D scene will be placed. Move the mouse while holding the middle
button. The interaction should be very intuitive. I 'borrowed' the
mouse input routines from the amesh demo program. They basically
extract azimuth and declination from the mouse x and y movements.
These values get used in a call to the 'polarview' gl function. Once
the mouse is released, the spectrogram will be redrawn in the new
orientation.

Every time a new orientation is indicated, subsequent data from the
file will be displayed using the new transformations. Once a file has
been completely displayed (or 'Length' slices of data, whichever is
smaller), the file is closed and, if the user requests that it be re-
displayed, re-opened and re-read. This way, the actually 3-D surface
is not actually stored. It turns out that the time required to
manipulate a set of say 300x512 points is so long as to make
interactivity pretty unlikely on most machines, so I went with this
'redraw' approach. The grid is a decent compromise, akin to dragging
outlined regions instead of all the RGB pixels in an image
application.

The sliders on the right affect the image as follows: The Translation
group allows you to translate  the center of the spectrogram display
in the time, amplitude, and frequency directions. The Cursor slider
lets you navigate through sub-sections of the surface when in
'Navigate' mode (see below).  Zoom gets translated into the 'field of
view' argument to the call to perspective. Gain affects how much the
incoming audio data is scaled before being rendered. This has two
effects on the image-- as the gain increases: the image gets taller,
and more of the lookup table locations are addressed. This is in
contrast with the 'ascale' slider which affects only the height, not
the color, of the image. Floor allows you to adjust the offset of the
fft magnitude, so, for example, you can just look at the peaks of the
spectral surface, or look down in the valleys as well. Together, gain
and floor are very useful for tweaking the display for any particular
image. Twist twirls the surface about the z-axis (only intuitive if
you haven't changed the Translation sliders).  It's just there for
completeness. Tscale, ascale, and fscale allow you to scale the
surface in each of the time, amplitude, and frequency directions,
respectively.

There are four menus available: File, Colors, Filter, and Window.
They can be opened up by clicking with any mouse button. To select
any of the options, you must use the right mouse button. To exit from
a menu selection without actually choosing an option, use the right
mouse button outside the menu. The File menu is what you use to open
new files, select live audio input, or to quit. Please use this
method to quit, so that the palette will be put back where it was
when the program was started.  If you forget to do this (or if the
program crashes!) you can reset the palette by issuing the command
'makemap' at the UNIX command line (or clicking the makemap icon).

The colored sliders on the left allow you to manipulate the color
lookup table. There are two modes of interaction, as selected by
using the 'Colors' menu. When 'Interpolate' is chosen, the sliders on
the left allow you to select the color that a color index of zero
(minimum amplitude) will map to, while those on the right are used to
select the color for color index 255 (maximum amplitude). The
remaining colors in the LUT are linearly interpolated. In
'SineColors' mode, the sliders are reinterpreted. The group on the
left gives Reg, Green, and Blue 'phase' values, while that on the
right gives the corresponding frequencies. Sinusoidal chrominance
gratings are thus created for each of R, G, and B, where the
gratings' frequencies and phases are independently specified. This is
a useful hack for visualizing surfaces that don't have much dynamic
range. For most images, it is most useful to keep the frequency
sliders very low and just tinker with the phases to obtain pleasing
palettes. Winning combinations can be saved using the SGI savemap
command from the shell. Subsequently, the Open option of the Colors
menu can be used to load one of your .map files into the palette.
Note that spectrogram assumes the existance of a 'map' directory just
as it assumes the existance of a 'sounds' directory. If you don't
have one, you can again over-ride it by clicking with the mouse on
the directory file of the dialog box.

The Filter menu allows you to load and save time-varying filters.
These are raw binary floating point files representing the series of
Fourier transform magnitudes used by spectrogram to do time-varying
filtering.  The size of this file is 4 * number_of_fft_frames *
(FFT_order/2 + 1).  The easiest way to create a .filt file (the
existance of a 'filt' directory is assumed- see Colors and File menu
comments above) is to analyze an interesting time varying sound
(SynWin should be enabled), go into 'Navigate' mode (see below), and
select Save from the Filter menu. A .filt file will be created of the
FFT magnitude data. This file can subsequently loaded via the Open
option of this menu. To apply the filter, select the Modify button.

The Window Menu allows you to select from eight different window types
that have proven useful in doing audio analysis. They basically trade
off resolution vs sidelobe suppression in the resulting FFT. There
is an option to turn off windowing (i.e. rectangular windowing), which
gives the best resolution but also has terrible sidelobe artifacts. If
you are going to do time varying filters, Hanning or Hamming windows 
are recommended.

The Length window, mentioned briefly above, allows you to type in the
number of slices you would like to see. This is useful if you don't
want to see an entire file. It comes up with a number computed from
the length of the .aiff file, or 500 if live input is selected.  For
the polar mode display (see below) this number should be at least
1000.

There are many buttons which control various features. The most
important one is labelled Pause underneath. The button itself is
labelled according to whether we are reading from Live Input, reading
from a File Input, or in Pause (also known as 'Navigate' mode). Pause
will freeze the display until it is clicked again, causing the button
to revert back to it's original (LiveIn or FileIn) state. Once you
are in a navigate mode, the current surface can be examined in detail
using the Length window and the Cursor slider. The Cursor slider
indicates the first slice to be shown (the slider range can be set by
clicking on the number boxes at either end). The length tells how
many slices, as before.  The mode used to render the surface can be
changed by clicking on the 'Mesh' button. Each time it is clicked,
the drawing style will cycle through Mesh, Line, Point, and Polygon
rendering methods. Line and Point modes render more quickly, so
consider using them while 'roaming around'. Depending on your machine,
Mesh or Poly are the next fastest, so experiment. 


Clear clears and redraws the screen. It also flushes any audio
samples that are waiting to be read from the A/D convertor, so you
can use this to 'catch up' with your live input, if things are
falling behind.  Impulse and modify are useful only when you are in
navigate mode and have loaded a filter (the program boots with a slow
sweep from high to low frequency in place). Impulse will substitute 
the STFT of a train of impulses for the surface analyzed from
the input signal. This is useful in conjunction with Modify, since it
allows you to hear the time-varying filter's impulse response. Modify
applies the currently active filter to the current input's transform
(or to the transform of an impulse train).

Perspective allows you to disable the perspective transformation, so
that distant things will be the same size as close ones. This can be
useful if you just want a straight waterfall display for example.
'ZB' and 'DB' enable Z-buffering and Double buffering, respectively.
On 8-bit machines, double buffering will completely trash your
colors, since you end up with two 4-bit buffers. The LogF button
allows the frequency axis to be scaled logarithmically instead of
linearly. Useful for applications requiring higher resolution in the
lower frequencies. Polar will create a novel '3D radar screen'
effect. This is a neat way of getting around the fact that eventually
we 'run off the end' when we are rendering slices linearly. Instead,
successive slices are rotated redially about the y axis. You even get
a different grid in this mode. The 2D button allows you to choose
between have a 2D or a 3D display.  This mode can display some
beautiful details which might not be obvious in  all 3D displays.
Clicking a second time returns to 3D mode. Clicking on the Axes
button will place you in Axes mode, where all axes will be labelled.
The time axis is labelled in seconds, the frequency axis is in kHz,
and the amplitude is labelled in dB.

>From the smaller button group: Monitor will turn on and off the
output of audio to the speaker. In LiveIn and FileIn mode, this is
just a copy of the audio as it's being analyzed. In Pause mode, it
actually is resynthesized by taking Inverse FFT's and doing the
overlap add computation. Scope will bring up a time domain display of
what's going on. This is really useful in understanding how the
overlap add computation works. It only works when you're
resynthesizing (in Pause mode). The top trace is the IFFT of current
frame, the middle trace is the IFFT of the previous frame, and the
bottom trace is the sum of the overlapping samples, which gets sent
to the DAC. You may not be able to do this and keep up with the DAC
sample rate.  Meter gives a running status (to the right of the 3D
plot) of how many audio input samples are in the input buffer waiting
to be read.  This shows whether or not the rendering is keeping up
with a given audio data rate. If the bar is increasing over time, you
should try a slower A/D sampling rate, or smaller overlap amount
(greater stride). Env tells the program to plot the 'spectral
envelope' of the input, instead of the normal FFT plot. This spectral
envelope is computed by performing a 12th order LPC analysis, and
taking the FFT of the impulse response of the LPC synthesis filter.
The surface so created can be saved via the filter menu (go into Pause
mode first), and used subsequently as a time-varying filter.

Lastly, the number boxes at the left allow you to input parameters
for the type of FFT analysis you want. You can set the FFT size, the
window size, and the stride. FFT size must be a power of 2. Win Size
can be anything up to FFT size. This is the length of the window
applied to the audio data before applying the FFT. Shorter windows
give faster dynamics, but poorer frequency resolution. Stride refers
to the number of samples to step ahead in the input data after each
analysis frame. In general, the stride should be set to some
submultiple of the window size (one half is good), and the window
size should be as long as possible for a given FFT length. If you are
going to do modifications, you may find objectionable artifacts (time
domain aliasing) if you set the window length too long. Longer FFT's
will require more of your CPU, since the FFT is O(N log N)
computations.

There are many command line arguments. To read about them, just type
spectrogram -h (for help), and a list of options will be printed. A
useful parameter is -O, which will cause the file 'testout.aiff' to
be created in the current directory. All samples that are synthesized
(not those that are being analyzed- in other words, only sounds
generated in navigate mode) also get written to this file. This is
very useful if you want to operate at higher sample rates than your
machine can support in real time.

A little tweaking goes a long way. Have fun, and thanks for trying it out!

Alan Peevers
